export default "This guide is for educational purposes only and is not a substitute for medical advice.\n\n## Your First Cycles: What to Expect\n\nGetting your first period is often described as a milestone, but the first few years of periods can feel anything but predictable. That is completely normal. The hormonal system that governs the menstrual cycle takes time to calibrate.\n\nIn the first 2 to 3 years after your first period, it is normal for cycles to:\n- Vary significantly in length from month to month\n- Skip a month (or two)\n- Be shorter or longer than the \"typical\" 28 days\n- Have irregular flow, lighter one month, heavier the next\n\nThis variability is not a sign that something is wrong. The pituitary gland, ovaries, and uterus are still establishing their working rhythm. By the time most people are in their mid-to-late teens, cycles tend to settle into a more consistent personal pattern.\n\n## Why Teen Cycles Are Different\n\nIn early adolescence, many cycles are anovulatory. The uterus sheds its lining even though ovulation did not occur. This happens because the hormonal signal between the brain and the ovaries is still maturing. Without ovulation, there is no luteal phase progesterone, and the cycle timing and flow can be less predictable.\n\nAs the hormonal system matures, ovulatory cycles become more frequent, and the cycle typically becomes more consistent.\n\nThis is also why fertility awareness methods (like tracking ovulation for contraception) are not appropriate for teens with irregular cycles. The patterns are too variable to use accurately.\n\n## What Is Actually Worth Tracking\n\nYou do not need to track everything. Start simple:\n\n### Essential\n- **First day of your period each month.** This is the most useful single data point. It tells you your cycle length when you look at the gap between period starts.\n- **Last day of bleeding (or approximately).** This gives you period duration.\n\n### Helpful additions\n- **Flow:** Light, moderate, heavy. A quick one-word note each day of your period is enough.\n- **Cramping:** Whether you had it, how bad it was on a scale that makes sense to you.\n- **Symptoms you notice:** Headaches, mood changes, bloating, spotting between periods.\n\nThat is it to start. You can add more detail over time if you want it.\n\n### What not to worry about\nYou do not need to track every potential symptom, rate your mood on a 10-point scale daily, or maintain a perfect record. An imperfect log with some missed days is still useful. The goal is a general picture of your cycle over several months, not a complete medical record.\n\n## How to Tell if Something Is Wrong\n\nMost period irregularity in the teen years is normal. But some things are worth getting checked out.\n\n**Talk to a doctor if:**\n- Your cramps are severe enough to prevent you from going to school or doing normal activities\n- Over-the-counter pain relief (like ibuprofen) does not help your cramps\n- You are bleeding heavily enough to soak through a pad or tampon in under an hour\n- You are bleeding for more than 7 days in a row\n- You are passing clots larger than a quarter\n- You are seeing spotting or bleeding between your periods that is not just light discharge\n- You have not had a period by age 16, or it has been more than 90 days since your last one and you have been having periods for more than 2 years\n\nHeavy periods in teens can sometimes be caused by a bleeding disorder called von Willebrand disease. If heavy periods are a consistent problem, getting tested is reasonable and can make a significant difference in how they are managed.\n\n## Period Tracking and Privacy\n\nThis section matters specifically for teens.\n\nMany popular period tracker apps require an account and upload your data to their servers. This means the company has records of your period dates, symptoms, and any other information you log. That data is not covered by HIPAA. Companies can share it with advertisers, as Flo Health was found to have done with Facebook and Google in 2021. It can potentially be accessed by parents through shared accounts, family cloud services, or app store purchase histories.\n\nIf you want your period tracking to stay private:\n\n- Choose an app that stores data only on your device, with no account required\n- Be aware that apps connected to a family Apple ID or Google account may show purchase or download history even if the app data itself is local\n- A simple paper notebook or calendar works well and has no data exposure at all\n\nYour period is your health information. You have the right to track it privately, and you have the right to choose tools that respect that.\n\n## Talking to a Doctor (Without It Being Awkward)\n\nYou do not need a parent in the room to see a doctor about your period. In most states, minors can receive confidential reproductive health care. If you are seeing a provider about your cycle, having 2 to 3 months of tracking data makes the conversation much easier. You can say \"my cycles have been between 24 and 45 days\" rather than \"I don't know, they're irregular.\"\n\nDoctors who specialize in adolescent gynecology are experienced with exactly this: early period patterns, pain management, and building a picture of your cycle health. Severe cramps, very heavy periods, and significant irregularity beyond the typical early-cycle adjustment period are all worth bringing up, not dismissing.";
